    ">\n",
    "> **(b)** State fixed effects absorb all characteristics of a state that are constant over time — geography, culture, climate, institutional history, etc. They allow us to compare changes *within* each state over time, rather than comparing levels across states. **(a) is wrong** because it describes state-specific time trends (which allow each state its own slope over time), a more demanding specification than simple fixed effects. **(c) is wrong** because it describes an interaction term between treatment and time, which is the DD coefficient itself, not a fixed effect. **(d) is wrong** because fixed effects do not eliminate measurement error in the dependent variable — they only remove time-invariant confounders.\n",
